Subject: PassReturn: constructors of its own, for the iOS SDK's libc++

A default member initializer of a struct nested in CommandBuffer is not usable while CommandBuffer is being defined;
the SDK's libc++ 15 then finds the deleter not default-constructible and refuses unique_ptr's own default
constructor (the host's newer libc++ did not mind). Found by the bundle build; the host tests are as they were.


---
diff --git a/hakios-metal/include/hakios-metal/command.hpp b/hakios-metal/include/hakios-metal/command.hpp
index 2acd2e1..a08c155 100644
--- a/hakios-metal/include/hakios-metal/command.hpp
+++ b/hakios-metal/include/hakios-metal/command.hpp
@@ -361,7 +361,11 @@ private:
     // its register shadows have taken from the heap (render.cpp): a frame is eight passes or so, and each made those
     // again from nothing, a doubling at a time.
     struct PassReturn {
-        CommandQueue* queue = nullptr;
+        // (constructors of its own: a member initializer here is no use to unique_ptr while CommandBuffer itself is still
+        // being defined, and the iOS SDK's libc++ then finds no default constructor for the deleter)
+        PassReturn() : queue(nullptr) {}
+        explicit PassReturn(CommandQueue* q) : queue(q) {}
+        CommandQueue* queue;
         void operator()(OpenPass* p) const;
     };
     using OpenPassPtr = std::unique_ptr<OpenPass, PassReturn>;
